    We are no longer assuming Rs(T) = Rinf(C)
    tau R' + R = Rinf
    tau = 22

    Rewriting the equation using integrating factor method
    R' + (1/Tau) * R = Rinf(t) / Tau
    u(x) = e^(integral(1/tau) dt)
    => u(x) e^(t/22)
    R(t) = 1/e^(t/22) * (integral (e^(t/22) * Rinf(t)/22 dt) + C)

    Linearity:
    By definition of linear ODE's this equation is a first order 
    linear differential equation. Theirfore it has linearity

    Time-Invarience: 
    h(z(t)) = z(t) - 22 * R'(t)
    h(rinf(t - t0)) = rinf(t - t0) - 22 * R'(t)
    R(t - t0) = rinf(t - t0) - 22*R'(t - t0)
    Time Varying, not as h(rinf(t-t0)) != R(t - t0)

    Causality:
    r(t) = rinf(t) - 22r'(t)
    r(t - t0) = rinf(t - t0) - 22r'(t - t0)
    r(-t) = rinf(-t) - 22r'(-t)
    r(-t - t0) = rinf(t - t0) - 22r'(t - t0)
    Equation appears to be causal dependant on only current values of 
    T and past values. I emphasize here that we are defining the 
    derivitve in terms of its backword propogation. 
        lim dt -> 0, X'(t) = (X(t) - X(t - dt))/dt

